<ResearchGuideLayout
  title="Research Product Documentation: SKU, Lot and COA Guide"
  description="Understand how research product names, SKUs, lot numbers, configurations, labels, inventory records, and Certificates of Analysis work together."
  eyebrow="DOCUMENTATION GUIDE"
  heading="Research product documentation, in detail."
  introduction="A well-organized product record connects the catalog page, selected configuration, SKU, label, lot number, availability, and any published Certificate of Analysis."
>
  <p>Research product documentation is easier to use when every identifier has a clear purpose. Product names help with discovery, while SKUs and lot numbers provide more precise links between commerce, inventory, labels, and laboratory records.</p>

  <h2>The identifiers you will see</h2>
  <ol class="rgv-guide-steps">
    <li><div><strong>Product name</strong><span>The public catalog name used to find and recognize the research item.</span></div></li>
    <li><div><strong>SKU</strong><span>A stock-keeping identifier used to distinguish the product or a specific configuration in the catalog.</span></div></li>
    <li><div><strong>Configuration</strong><span>The selected format or variation associated with price, stock, and order records.</span></div></li>
    <li><div><strong>Lot number</strong><span>A batch-level identifier that connects a physical label to its available laboratory record.</span></div></li>
    <li><div><strong>COA record</strong><span>A laboratory document associated with the product and, when available, a particular lot.</span></div></li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Why the SKU and lot are different</h2>
  <p>A SKU usually identifies what is sold or stocked. A lot number identifies a particular documented batch. Multiple units with the same SKU may come from different lots over time, which is why both identifiers matter.</p>
  <p>Use the <a href="/research-guides/find-a-coa-by-lot-number">guide to finding a COA by lot number</a> to match a physical label to its original report.</p>

  <h2>What to review before ordering</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Product identity:</strong> confirm the name and catalog page match the intended research record.</li>
    <li><strong>Available configuration:</strong> review the selected format and its current inventory status.</li>
    <li><strong>Documentation:</strong> open the COA library when a product or lot record is available.</li>
    <li><strong>Research-use labeling:</strong> confirm the item is appropriate for qualified laboratory and in-vitro research workflows only.</li>
    <li><strong>Order record:</strong> retain the confirmation number so support can locate the transaction efficiently.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="rgv-guide-callout">
    <strong>Documentation is product-specific</strong>
    <p>Do not treat a similarly named item, a different configuration, or another lot as interchangeable documentation. Match the identifiers shown on the catalog, order, label, and report.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Where to find each record</h2>
  <p>The <a href="/shop">research catalog</a> displays product and configuration information. The <a href="/coa">COA library</a> provides available laboratory records, and the <a href="/account">customer portal</a> keeps order history connected to your account.</p>
</ResearchGuideLayout>
